{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>At the end of seven years you shall make a release.</b> The commentators say that the expression מקץ ('at the end of') begins the seven years, for every thing has two ends, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 26:28): ״מן הקצה אל הצצה״ — 'from one end to the other,' and both the beginning and the end are each called קצה. But this is not the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory; rather, מקץ שבע שנים means at the end of seven years, for Scripture did not say מקץ השנה השביעית — 'at the end of the seventh year' — for had it said so, its sense would be the beginning; but now that it says מקץ שבע שנים, its sense is the other end, namely the end of seven years. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the Sabbatical year does not release loans except at its end, and so Onkelos translated: 'at the end.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory received a tradition that the Temple was destroyed at the outgoing of the Sabbatical year, for they expounded in Arakhin: 'They roll merit for a meritorious day and liability for a liable day.' When the First Temple was destroyed, it was at the outgoing of Shabbat and at the outgoing of the Sabbatical year and on the eve of the ninth of Av. And likewise regarding the Second Temple they said: 172 years after the destruction of the Temple, the four thousand were completed — for in the year 3821 the Temple was destroyed according to the words of Rabbenu Tam of blessed memory, which are the authoritative reckoning, and even though most of that year had passed in building, since in that year the Temple was destroyed, we count it among the years of destruction. And we have today 51 years for the reckoning of creation; add 172 to 51 and they are 223; take two from every hundred (i.e., for the Jubilee cycles), as we say in the Gemara of Avodah Zarah; since all the remainder are Sabbatical years, there come from the thousand and the two hundred 24, and the fiftieth year counts both here and here, for the reckoning of Jubilee and for the reckoning of Shemittah; add those 24 to the 23 remaining and they are 47; make Shemittot of them — 5 remain. You find that for two years it would be Shemittah, and this was in year 53, and likewise in year 60, and after it 67, and after it 74. And according to the view of Rashi of blessed memory, that the Temple was destroyed in year 3820, the Shemittah would be year 52, and after it 59, and after it 66, and after it 73." "<b>And the expression</b> תעשה שמטה ('you shall make a release') is like the expression לעשות את יום השבת ('to make the day of Shabbat'), and the intention is that there shall be cessation here and here — on the day of Shabbat from labor, and in the year of Shemittah from working the land. And the matter is one in the sanctity of sevenths: this one in days and that one in years, and the Jubilee among the Shemittot. And this has already been explained in the section of Behar Sinai."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase שקול מכל מאה תרתי literally means 'two are taken from every hundred'; the subsequent arithmetic works from 223 by removing 2×2=4 for two complete centuries, yielding 219, then dividing 219 by 7 yields 31 Shemittot (217 years) with 2 remainder, plus adjustments described for the 50th year — the passage preserves Rabbeinu Bahya's exact figures as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:29.141932581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}